\DOC SIMD_SIMPLIFY_ABBREV_TAC

\TYPE {SIMD_SIMPLIFY_ABBREV_TAC : thm list -> thm list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Simplifies the most recently introduced assumption of the goal, typically an
equation giving the value of a vector register, rewriting SIMD word operations
into a reduced form and leaving the conclusion unchanged.

\DESCRIBE
Takes two theorem-list arguments: {unfold_defs}, equational theorems whose
left-hand sides are unfolded during simplification, and {unfold_aux}, further
equational theorems serving as auxiliary rewrites for combinations of those
definitions with the surrounding word operations. The tactic acts on one
assumption, replacing it by a simplified version and moving it to the end of
the assumption list (so the preceding assumption shifts up by one); all other
assumptions and the conclusion are untouched. The simplification collapses
nested extraction/packing of lanes (subword-of-join style terms) so that the
right-hand side of the assumption is stated directly in terms of the individual
lane values. In many applications the term is already reduced, so the only
visible effect is the positional change, and {t'} may be identical to {t}.
{
   A, t ?- u
   =========================================  SIMD_SIMPLIFY_ABBREV_TAC unfold_defs unfold_aux
   A, t' ?- u
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces the goal has the same shape — a machine-state
{eventually} conclusion with a long list of state-reading assumptions — and the
same pair of theorem-list arguments is supplied.

\ENDDOC
